WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.640
Peter Desberg, author/screenwriter: Penultimate
questions.

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:05.669
Jeffrey Davis, author/screenwriter:: That’s
okay because she’s so adorable.

00:00:05.669 --> 00:00:08.440
Peter: You know that we’re judging your
questions, right?

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:11.790
Film Courage: …That’s okay and I’m at
peace with that

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:14.440
Jeffrey: That’s good because I’m not.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:17.460
Peter: Did you have enough carrot juice to
make you at peace with that?

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.179
Film Courage: Yes, I did actually have enough
carrot juice.

00:00:19.179 --> 00:00:20.789
Jeffrey Davis: 92 references to carrot juice.

00:00:20.789 --> 00:00:23.929
Film Courage: I actually have to go back and
get more.

00:00:23.929 --> 00:00:27.070
I’m running low…what are some…[laughs].

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:30.980
Peter: See another multiple parter “what
are some?”

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:32.570
Go ahead.

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:38.800
Film Courage: What are some of the most important
questions a screenwriter can ask themselves

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.000
when developing a story idea?

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.280
Peter: How much is it worth?

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:44.280
[Laughs]

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:49.440
Jeffrey: A point for each, that doesn’t
get you to a hundred.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:57.170
Peter:&nbsp; I think one important question is
how universal is it?

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:03.429
You know, you may be having the most esoteric
story but if it touches everybody it’s a

00:01:03.429 --> 00:01:04.530
universal story.

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:08.100
If it’s very, very narrow, then it doesn’t.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:14.200
So you’re always saying how are people going
to relate can anyone find something in this

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:18.340
relationship I’m creating, in this conflict
that I’m creating that they can recognize

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:19.340
something.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:24.890
Film Courage: That’s why you asked the Mother-Daughter
premise for the book (Now That’s Funny)

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:26.299
because you figured most people can relate.

00:01:26.299 --> 00:01:31.040
Jeffrey: It’s a generic old premise starting
with my sister Eileen.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.500
Peter: Everybody has had a mother.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:38.409
Jeffrey: I think it was chosen because you…

00:01:38.409 --> 00:01:40.340
Peter: Well I don’t know your background?

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:41.340
Jeffrey: Because you could…

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:42.340
Peter: Poor teacher…

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:46.200
Jeffrey: Well she’s a child of a single
mother.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.409
She told us if you’d been listening.

00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:52.750
I have a slightly different take on that.

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:57.950
I believe in the Stephen Sondheim dictum that
the more personal a story is (and I don’t

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:04.820
mean autobiographical) that the more it matter
to you the most universal its going to be.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:06.219
But both things are valid.

00:02:06.219 --> 00:02:07.719
It depends on the writer.

00:02:07.719 --> 00:02:14.730
I will tell you that if you try to write something
commercial you will fail every time.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:23.150
For example, when DIE HARD was a success there
was one movie after another that came out

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:32.409
with that kind of…none of them was as good
as DIE HARD but for every movie that came

00:02:32.409 --> 00:02:39.000
out like that (hero in a trapped place trying
to save the world - whatever that is).

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.620
Peter: With a sense of humor.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.590
Jeffrey: With a sense of humor, that’s very
important.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:49.290
There were hundreds of scripts that were written
that never saw the light of anywhere.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:52.670
I believe you have to write what you want
to write.

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:56.870
Peter: Look at what “Go ahead, make my day”
did.

00:02:56.870 --> 00:02:58.450
Everybody tried to compete with that.

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:03.069
Jeffrey: And that wasn’t a new concept when
it was written.

00:03:03.069 --> 00:03:08.310
I mean BULLET worked (say concept).

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:11.460
BULLET was a more stylish cop.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.190
The Dirty Harry movies were great.

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:18.860
They’re well made, they are kind of the
detective version of the spaghetti westerns.

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:22.569
Peter: But having the one key line at the
right moment.

00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:26.830
Jeffrey: But today I don’t think one key
line is going to make a great movie.

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:36.260
I think it was the right actor at the right
time for the late sixties.

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:41.689
But I think the other thing you need is patience…well
all the things we’ve been talking about.

00:03:41.689 --> 00:03:43.000
The ability to tolerate…

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:44.629
Peter: [Whispers] Ambiguity.

00:03:44.629 --> 00:03:45.720
Jeffrey: What?

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:47.360
Peter: [Whispers] Ambiguity.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:48.450
Jeffrey: Ambiguity.

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:50.180
He likes that word.

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:53.019
Nobody else understands it.

00:03:53.019 --> 00:03:54.629
The need to tolerate failure.

00:03:54.629 --> 00:03:59.180
The need to get up and try it the next day.

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:04.900
No writing career can ever be made on one
script.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:11.269
I really believe that you’ve got to write
a lot and not judge it (we’ve talked of

00:04:11.269 --> 00:04:14.370
all this before).

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:17.440
And then of course be willing to show your
work.

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:19.730
Film Courage: Not waiting ten years?

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:24.220
Jeffrey: If you’re just going to put your
work in the drawer and you’re just doing

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:26.020
it for your own pleasure, that’s fine.

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:28.950
But don’t say you want to be a professional
writer.

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:31.820
Peter: I’m waiting for the nineteen part
last question.

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:34.470
Film Courage: Well there was a follow-up to
something you said.

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:35.470
Jeffrey: Okay.

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:36.470
Peter: Oh, that’s so cheating!

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:38.650
Film Courage: And the follow-up is…

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:40.410
Peter: Penultimate part two?

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:45.600
Film Courage: You said that most films can
be based on or their popularity could be based

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.530
on one line…make my day or…

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:50.490
Jeffrey: You mean the hook?

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:52.900
Film Courage: Or “Frankly my dear, I don’t
give a damn.”

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:53.900
Something like that

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:56.750
Jeffrey: Yes, but that had nothing to do with
the plot of the movie, that’s just…

00:04:56.750 --> 00:04:57.750
Peter: Character.

00:04:57.750 --> 00:04:58.960
Jeffrey: That’s just character.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:01.750
Film Courage: But you don’t feel that today?

00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:07.040
Jeffrey: Well they want a hook, before they
want to hear the premise, before they want

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:08.570
to hear the story.

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:15.920
So in something we’re working on now we
used BACK TO THE FUTURE, the hook would be

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:25.090
a young boy goes back time to make sure his
parents get married so he’ll continue to

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:26.090
exist.

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:30.810
That’s terrible, I cant remember what we
used, that’s a hook, that’s not a premise,

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:31.810
yet.

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:40.720
It’s an idea for…so to me it’s an idea
for a story.

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:42.980
You still haven’t developed the story.

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:48.430
The thing you brought in about the two brothers
is much more developed by that.

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:53.290
Film Courage: But in terms of a line sort
of living on past the film.

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:57.461
Jeffrey: Oh, oh, you mean why do lines live
on past the film.

00:05:57.461 --> 00:05:58.720
Film Courage: Are we beyond that?

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:06.370
Jeffrey: No I don’t think we’re ever beyond
that, I mean you get memorable lines as recently

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:07.720
as WHEN HARRY MET SALLY?

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:08.720
What’s the line?

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:10.330
Film Courage: “I’ll have what she’s
having.”

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:14.490
Jeffrey: Exactly, you know that line was improved,
it wasn’t even a scripted line.

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:16.520
That was Rob Reiner’s mom, that’s Carl
Reiner’s wife.

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:19.060
Film Courage: But wasn’t that from the nineties?

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:20.260
So anything today?

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:22.380
Jeffrey: Oh you’re saying now?

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:23.560
Yes!

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:29.370
There is one in a recent movie, I don’t
know have you guys seen GET OUT recently?

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:30.730
Film Courage: Not yet.

00:06:30.730 --> 00:06:31.990
It’s on the list.

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:36.460
Peter: I think writers are always trying to
write those lines but they are usually really

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:38.250
self-conscious and awkward.

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:43.080
Jeffrey: This wasn’t because this was a
movie…the only thing I can tell you about

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:48.740
the movie, it was made for nothing and the
guy was famous before he made it, the director’s

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:50.320
wonderful, Jordan Peele.

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:58.300
He had that show on Comedy Central and he
knows everybody.

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:03.980
But this his directorial debut and the movie
wasn’t expected to do well and it was one

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:08.410
of the most successful movies of the year
and there is a great line in it that I think

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:15.190
will live on past the movie and that’s…the
premise is a white girl takes her black boyfriend

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:19.740
to meet the parents…that’s not what the
movie is about at all but that’s all I can

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:21.910
tell you or it will ruin the movie for you.

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:27.420
But the first thing the father says to the
black boyfriend who is actually played to

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:33.300
my wife’s consternation by a British actor
[Daniel Kaluuya], he says “You know I would

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:36.090
have voted for Obama for a third term.”

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:42.380
Now that line is brilliant, it’s also in
character, it has to do with the movie but

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:48.290
it’s so witty and it’s so true that like
liberals (embarrassed liberals) when they

00:07:48.290 --> 00:07:54.330
want to say something good and they’re…it’s
part of what the movie is about that people

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:59.910
will…he said in an interview that he gets
that all the time from white liberals that

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:05.960
they wished he could have had a third term,
they are trying to show you how liberal they

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:06.960
are.

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:12.630
So I think Peter is right have those just
like “We’ll always have Paris.”

00:08:12.630 --> 00:08:17.260
Peter: But it’s just bad when you see it
self-consciously written like I think I’ve

00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:19.230
just written that line and [grimaces].

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:26.550
Jeffrey: Well hopefully you are a good enough
writer to see that and take it out.

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:31.870
The other thing that really bugs me in writing
is when the writer makes a reference that

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:36.900
obviously doesn’t belong in the period or
they are trying to be too contemporaneous,

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:42.551
they’re trying to be too hip, so they’ll
make a joke about something they’ll make

00:08:42.551 --> 00:08:46.500
a joke about something nobody would have made
a joke about.

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:47.710
I see that all the time.

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:52.110
I’ve probably done it many times.

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:57.260
But again a great line is great, it’s great
for advertising, but it doesn’t make a great

00:08:57.260 --> 00:08:58.260
movie.

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:03.570
There are so many lines in DIE HARD, but that’s
not why the movie is successful.

00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:07.740
I have a theory about why that movie is unique
and successful.

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:14.170
He wasn’t there to save the world, he was
there to get his wife out of the building.

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:17.020
And that’s what made it human.

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:25.690
And a lot of these like SPEED which was a
big success which followed it was such a contrived

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:31.780
situation, a bus that if it goes below a certain
[speed limit] it will blow up.

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:34.120
I mean, come on?

00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:39.400
And then they manipulate by having the old
trick which everybody has used of having their

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:46.190
partner hurt which raised the stakes, which
can work, it worked great for…oh come on…help

00:09:46.190 --> 00:09:52.780
me out…for Lillian Hellman’s boyfriend
who was blacklisted…you know a great mystery

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:53.780
writer.

00:09:53.780 --> 00:09:54.850
Peter: It’s been used in a zillion movies.

00:09:54.850 --> 00:09:55.850
Jeffrey: Yeah, but who was it?

00:09:55.850 --> 00:10:05.330
He was the guy who did it better than everybody,
the movie about the birds…you know where

00:10:05.330 --> 00:10:10.050
there was something in the "The stuff that
dreams are made of,” a classic line that

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:13.990
we’ll always know and that’s from THE
MALTESE FALCON.

00:10:13.990 --> 00:10:16.650
Film Courage: What line?

00:10:16.650 --> 00:10:25.590
Peter: You know when he sees what’s inside
the bird, the stones or whatever, it’s the

00:10:25.590 --> 00:10:29.520
MacGuffin that everybody has been looking
for and he says “Ah, the stuff that dreams

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:30.710
are made of.”

00:10:30.710 --> 00:10:34.030
It’s great right?

00:10:34.030 --> 00:10:37.570
And then the other line in that movie that
I love is “I’m not going over for you

00:10:37.570 --> 00:10:40.460
sweetheart.”

00:10:40.460 --> 00:10:43.020
Meaning I’m not going to jail for you.

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:44.930
I love that movie, it’s so well-written.

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:48.180
Dashiell Hammett…thank you for helping me
so much.

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:50.440
Peter: I’m always there for you.

